  "meta": {
    "title": "Long division by 3",
    "blurb": "Reads a binary number most-significant bit first and writes its quotient by 3, one bit per bit read. The states are the remainder — exactly the divisibility-by-3 DFA — and each edge also says whether 3 went into the partial value.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "11",
        "out": "01",
        "label": "3 ÷ 3 = 1"
      },
      {
        "w": "110",
        "out": "010",
        "label": "6 ÷ 3 = 2"
      },
      {
        "w": "1111",
        "out": "0101",
        "label": "15 ÷ 3 = 5"
      },
      {
        "w": "1010",
        "out": "0011",
        "label": "10 ÷ 3 = 3 r 1"
      },
      {
        "w": "100101",
        "out": "001100",
        "label": "37 ÷ 3 = 12 r 1"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "binary",
        "arithmetic",
        "division",
        "remainder"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "readme": "School long division, in base 2. With remainder *r* so far, reading bit *b* makes the partial value 2*r* + *b*; the next quotient bit is ⌊(2*r* + *b*)/3⌋ and the new remainder is (2*r* + *b*) mod 3. Since 2*r* + *b* ≤ 5, each quotient bit is 0 or 1.\n\nThe output has as many bits as the input, leading zeros included: **1111** (15) gives **0101** (5), and **1010** (10) gives **0011** (3) with the machine left in r1 — the remainder. Compare **Binary divisibility by 3**, which is this machine with the outputs erased and r0 made accepting."
    }
  }
}
